        "variation": "The five buildup frames show five ordered pen-down runs: frame 1 starts at the upper-left and carries the upper curve into the lower bowl without lifting; frame 2 restarts at the middle junction and sweeps right through the shoulder; frame 3 descends the inner stem top-to-bottom; frame 4 descends the trailing stem top-to-bottom; frame 5 draws the shirorekhā left-to-right, for four intervening lifts. Thomas Egenes, Learning the Sanskrit Alphabet, p. 12 (Maharishi International University Press, 1990), documents the shared base अ itself as a six-stroke traditional Sanskrit form, so the joined modern body is not universal. This learner path follows the five-run modern form and fits its joins and directions to Noto Sans Devanagari."
